FINDINGS:
1. [MINOR] src/cli.rs:138-141 — An undeclared **short** flag in value position is silently stored as the flag's value, unlike an undeclared long flag (which is rejected at 138-139) and unlike the top-level `-x` rejection at 160-161. `sys.parse([{name:"name", type:"text"}], ["--name", "-x"])` succeeds with `name == "-x"` while `["-x"]` alone raises `Unknown flag '-x'`. That is inconsistent with the phase's stated "a typo in value position is an unknown flag" rule and means the new short-flag path can still swallow a mis-typed switch. Fix: in the value branch, reject a `next` that starts with `-`, is not `-`, and fails `is_negative_number` as `Unknown flag '{next}'` (or explicitly document that short-flag-shaped values are allowed).
2. [MINOR] src/cli.rs:466-468 — `is_negative_number` uses `parse::<f64>().is_ok_and(|n| n.is_finite())`, so tokens that parse as non-finite floats (`-inf`, `-Infinity`, `-NaN`, `-1e400`) fall through to the unknown-flag branch at 160-161 instead of being handled like other numbers. `sys.parse([], ["-inf"])` therefore reports `Unknown flag '-inf'`, whereas the same text in value position reports the finite-number failure. No test covers the `Ok`-but-non-finite path of this branch. Fix: either accept the token as a positional and let later conversion refuse it, or narrow the predicate to a numeric spelling and add a test for `-inf`/`-1e400` in both positions.
3. [MINOR] src/cli.rs:418-421 — In `from_default`, the `Kind::Number` arm copies `Value::Number(*number)` without routing it through `finite_number`, while `from_text` at 385 does. If a non-finite `Value::Number` ever reaches a declaration (the parser refuses non-finite literals, but the invariant is not enforced here), `sys.parse` will return an infinite/default value instead of the finite-number refusal the phase advertises. Fix: call `crate::value::finite_number(*number, span)?` in that arm, mirroring `from_text`. Reachability is unverified; this is a defensive gap and a test with a non-finite default would settle it.
4. [MINOR] src/runtime.rs:3753 — The `sys_parse` arm reads only `args.first()` and `args.get(1)` and never checks for extra arguments, so `sys.parse(options, [], "extra")` silently ignores the third argument rather than refusing it. The declaration and argument-list paths are rigorously validated, so silently accepting an extra value is inconsistent; if the generic builtin call path does not enforce arity, this is user-reachable. Fix: reject `args.len() > 2` with a `RuntimeError` naming the extra argument. Unverified whether an upstream arity check already exists.

REVIEW VERDICT: FINDINGS 4
